.TH CVEC_DECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_declare \- declare a vector variable and its companion type variables
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_declare(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable with the name v. That vector holds
elements of the given type. The macro also declares the two hidden
companion type variables. The type-dispatching macros read those variables
at each call site. The macro creates no vector. v stays uninitialized.
cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3) must give it a vector before any other
macro or function touches it.

Use this macro when the creation and the declaration must be separate. One
example is a choice of allocator that comes further down the function.
Another example is a variable at the top of a block, where the later paths
create it in different ways. cvec_construct(3) and cvec_construct_mp(3) do
both steps together when they do not need to be separate.
cvec_construct_scoped(3) and cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) also add automatic
destruction at the end of the scope.

The names of the companion variables come from v. The variables carry only
type information. Nothing reads them at run time, and they are marked
unused. A vector that goes only to the raw functions therefore costs
nothing. The value companion carries the element type. cvec_init(3) takes
the element size from it. cvec_at(3), cvec_push(3) and the other
type-dispatching macros also use it to type their result. The key companion
carries size_t. That is the type of the index which a vector iterator
reports through ccol_iter_key_ptr(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable to declare
type  Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
      Any type name is accepted, including a function
      pointer type such as void (*)(int) and an array
      type such as int[4]. For an array element type,
      give cvec_push(3) an array or a string literal.
      The macro copies its bytes into the element
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations. It needs a semicolon at
the end of the call site. It therefore belongs where declarations are
legal. It cannot be the body of an if or a loop without braces.
The companion variables must stay in scope everywhere that you use a
type-dispatching macro on v. cvec_redeclare(3) makes them again for a
handle that comes from another scope.
Destroy a vector from this macro with cvec_destroy(3), after the
initialization.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
v holds an indeterminate value until cvec_init(3) or cvec_init_mp(3)
gives it one. A read of v before that point is undefined behavior. This
includes a call to cvec_destroy(3) on a path that skipped the
initialization. cvec_declare_scoped(3) is the variant that starts at
NULL.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_declare(my_vec, int);
cvec_init(my_vec);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp (3),
.BR cvec_redeclare (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3)
